# SYNOPSIS

```usage
rc [-srdiIlxebpvV] [-c command] [-m rcmain] [file [arg ...]]
```

# DESCRIPTION

`rc` is the command interpreter: the shell.
It is Plan 9's `rc`, as 9front keeps it,, and is checked against it by running the same scripts through both.
It reads commands from a <file>, from the string given to `-c`, or from standard input, and runs them.

If `-c` is given, `rc` runs that <command> and ends.
Otherwise, if a <file> is given, `rc` runs it as a script: it is looked for along `$path`, `$0` is its name and `$*` the arguments after it.
A script starting `#!` is run by the interpreter it names, as `exec` would.
Otherwise `rc` reads standard input.

`rc` prompts, with `$prompt(1)` for a command and `$prompt(2)` while one is unfinished, only when it is interactive: when `-i` is given, or when it is on a terminal and `-I` is not.
It is on a terminal when `$TERM` is set or when it was started on a pty.
A command that is typed incomplete (an open brace or quote, a condition without its body) is continued on the next line.

When `rc` ends, its status is its exit string: none if the status is true, else `$status` (`false` if that is empty).

The flags are read as 9front's `getflags` reads them: letters may be grouped after one `-`, `-c` and `-m` take a value (the rest of their group, or the next argument), a letter given twice is an error, and the first argument not starting with `-` (or `-` alone) ends the flags.
Anything else prints the usage line and exits.

: `-c`
  The next argument is the command to run.
  `$cflag` holds it.
: `-e`
  A command that fails ends `rc`, unless it is in a condition (see {Control flow|#control}).
: `-x`
  Print each simple command, quoted as `whatis` would, on standard error before it runs.
: `-v`
  Print each line on standard error as it is read.
: `-s`
  Print `status=` and the status on standard error after each line that left a false one.
: `-i`
  Interactive, whatever standard input is.
: `-I`
  Not interactive, even on a terminal.
: `-l`
  A login `rc`: run `/lib/profile` first, if there is one.
: `-p`
  Take no functions from the environment, and set `$path` to `/bin` alone.
: `-r`, `-d`, `-b`, `-V`, `-m`
  Accepted and recorded for `flag`, with no other effect.
  There is no rcmain to name, and nothing to debug in the interpreter.

: `$rcname`
  `rc` sets it to the name it was run by.

